---
// Lesson 10.2e — Card Jam: Build + Playtest
// rpg jam: finish the card game; playtest round; AI fairness check.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.2e — Card Jam: Build + Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Both empty methods are full (<a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a>,
    <a href="10.2d.html">10.2d</a>). Today the card game becomes a
    GAME: win condition, balance, the first three matches of
    match-three classes of playtesting, and the fix list for
    tomorrow's showcase.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The finish list (in order):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Win/lose condition.</strong> Health to zero
            (your StatManager pattern), deck-out, or points —
            whatever your pitch (<a href="10.2a.html">10.2a</a>)
            promised. A game without an end is a toy — do not
            ship a toy.</li>
        <li><strong>The score UI</strong> — health numbers, energy,
            hand count: your HUD family from the boss bar
            (<a href="8.2.html">8.2</a>). Cards in hand must be
            CLICKABLE or draggable — the input is the game.</li>
        <li><strong>Balance pass.</strong> Play YOUR OWN game five
            times. Track win/loss: you should win maybe 70% — if
            you win 100% the AI is a speed bump, if you lose
            50% the AI is a wall. Tune the AI's score line or
            energy, ONE number per match (<a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>
            discipline, again, forever).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Card game classics</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Clicking a card plays it TWICE. Which event are
                you using (OnPointerDown vs OnPointerUp vs click
                + not-consumed)? Which one matches "player
                intent" best?</li>
            <li>The AI's hand renders on YOUR side of the screen
                after a reshuffle. Where did the render loop's
                parent transform go wrong (<a href="10.2c.html">10.2c</a>
                spawn-by-index — the index was fine; the PARENT
                was not)?</li>
            <li>Infinite loop: a deck that reshuffles discards
                into a deck that draws them again forever with
                no win progress. Which guard stops the loop —
                and is "no legal move = pass" a RULE or a
                BUG?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Three matches, three different partners, silent watching
    (<a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> rules, jam-scale
    <a href="10.1d.html">10.1d</a> rotation). Watch for the card
    game's specific tells:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Rule-check:</strong> the tester asks "can I play
            this?" — the card's PLAYABILITY is not visible
            enough (dim unplayable cards!)</li>
        <li><strong>The reading:</strong> they read every card out
            loud on first draw. That is normal ONCE — but note
            which card SILENCED them with confusion</li>
        <li><strong>Win understanding:</strong> at match end, did
            they know WHY they lost? Card games teach through
            loss — if the loss was a mystery, the resolution
            feedback failed</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Showcase fixes</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>One fix per playtest finding, applied and re-run
                (the 10.1d ritual, your default now).</li>
            <li>Build the binary + test the BUILD (<a href="10.1e.html">10.1e</a>
                freeze rule). Commit + push:
                <code>"10.2e: card game shippable"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Show a win condition, working input, and readable UI</li>
            <li>Report your own 5-match win rate and defend your AI tuning</li>
            <li>Diagnose the card-specific tells (playability, reading, loss understanding)</li>
            <li>Ship a tested build, not an editor run</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — build + test days</li>
        <li><a href="10.2a.html">10.2a — Kickoff</a> — your pitch card</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.2f.html">10.2f — Card Jam: Showcase</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.2e" title="10.2e Card Jam: Build + Playtest — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
